{
  "_note": "What semantics looks for. Edit this file to add event types, aspects or word lists; no code change. Event types are checked in order, first match wins (put specific ones first). 'speculative': true/false restricts a type to hedged/unhedged sentences. Every playbook trigger must be an event type here (checked at start-up). The same file can drive an LLM analyzer's labels. 'background' words mark a sentence as history, not news (no trigger). 'attribution' says where a claim comes from, so copies and re-reports are not counted as independent confirmation. 'commentary' = headline wording of opinion, explainer and feature pieces (\"Should you buy...\", \"Here's why...\", interviews). An article whose headline or opening sentence matches is commentary about news, not a new event, so none of its sentences open or extend an event. Checked against the live feed on 2026-11-16, where such pieces had been read as events. 'deal_*' = where a company's name stands in deal wording, giving its part: buyer, target or seller (semantics/logic.py deal_role). The two sides react differently (Andrade, Mitchell & Stafford 2001), so the expected share price move depends on it; checked on the 25 takeover events of 2026-11-17. Legal and dividend types (v9): a standalone regulator action or lawsuit, its outcome, and dividend or buyback changes, added after 41 legal and 11 capital return articles about our companies opened no event (2026-11-17). Law-firm adverts for class actions are commentary.",
  "version": "taxonomy-v9",
  "event_types": [
